## Pre-Step 2: Backend Hardening (before frontend contexts)

The frontend contexts are livestream-style WS subscribers. The server must be
rock-solid before we build that layer. Three phases:

### Phase A — WebSocket & Serialization Safety
- [ ] Remove all `.unwrap()` in WS handler (ws.rs) — log + skip on failure
- [ ] Remove `.expect()` calls in orchestrator.rs — Result propagation
- [ ] Audit all unwrap/expect in non-test server code
- [ ] Add broadcast `lagged` handling so slow clients don't block channels
- [ ] Tune per-channel buffer sizes (feed = high, stats = low)

### Phase B — Auth & API Protection
- [ ] Lock down CORS — read from `CORS_ORIGINS` env var, fail in production if unset
- [ ] Require `JWT_SECRET` in production — refuse to start without it
- [ ] Require WS authentication or cap unauthenticated connections
- [ ] Add HTTP rate limiting (tower-governor or similar)
  - Auth routes: 10 req/min per IP
  - General API: 100 req/min per user
  - WS inbound message rate limit

### Phase C — Operational Config
- [ ] DB pool size from `DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS` env var (default 10)
- [ ] Request ID middleware (tower-request-id) — log + response header
- [ ] Input validation: string length limits, enum checks, structured errors

